A database is an organized collection of digital data or information stored electronically in a computer system.
How Databases WorkStorage: Data is saved on physical hardware, such as hard drives or servers.
Management: A software layer called a Database Management System (DBMS) controls how data is accessed, updated, and secured.
Querying: Users and apps use languages like SQL (Structured Query Language) to search, sort, and modify the stored data quickly.
Common Types of DatabasesRelational Databases (SQL): Organize data into tables with rows and columns. Tables link to each other using keys. Examples include MySQL and PostgreSQL.
NoSQL Databases: Handle unstructured or flexible data without traditional tables. Examples include MongoDB.
Cloud Databases: Run on cloud computing platforms for remote access and scaling.
